1
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:03,232
John.

2
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:05,198
John.

3
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:12,155
Ladies and gentlemen of the jury,
have you reached a verdict?

4
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:13,639
We have, Your Honour.

5
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:16,633
Will the defendant please rise.

6
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,913
We find the defendant,
Lester Matthews, guilty of murder.

7
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:38,474
Les, we can appeal.

8
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:42,794
Les.

9
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:51,394
Thanks.

10
00:03:53,280 --> 00:03:54,315
Thanks a lot.

11
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:22,277
Relax.

12
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:24,237
I can't.

13
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:30,510
You know, I used to be lucky in life.

14
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:32,557
No matter what I did,
it turned out okay.

15
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:36,599
The last couple of years have been
one lousy turn after another.

16
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:41,989
Let's go, Les. They're ready.

17
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:04,591
Les, we considered your application
for parole very carefully.

18
00:05:04,760 --> 00:05:06,796
Frankly, we were impressed.

19
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:08,234
Your war record in Vietnam,

20
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:11,597
the fact that you remained a partner
with the Dacoma Corporation.

21
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:12,954
But most importantly,

22
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:16,271
your exemplary record here in
the seven years since your conviction.

23
00:05:16,440 --> 00:05:20,353
Look, John Colter was my best friend
and my partner. I did not murder him.

24
00:05:20,520 --> 00:05:23,796
The issue here is
your qualifications for parole,

25
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:26,474
not the circumstances
of your conviction.

26
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:30,428
Now, on that basis,
parole is granted.

27
00:05:36,720 --> 00:05:40,156
Thank you. Thank you.

28
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:44,392
I told you.

29
00:05:44,560 --> 00:05:46,516
Yeah,
I guess my luck's changing, huh?

30
00:05:46,680 --> 00:05:50,036
I'm really happy for you, Les.
I really am.

31
00:05:50,560 --> 00:05:52,835
Thanks, pal. Thanks a lot.

32
00:05:53,720 --> 00:05:56,951
Hey, lighten up, Eli.
Look, you'll be out of here in no time.

33
00:05:57,120 --> 00:06:01,079
Come on. Eighteen months
is like a lifetime in this joint.

34
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:04,676
I won't forget you, Les.

35
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:06,599
I won't let you.

36
00:06:06,760 --> 00:06:09,558
Look, I'll have a job waiting for you
when you get out.

37
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:11,312
You mean that?

38
00:06:12,120 --> 00:06:13,951
Would I lie to you?

39
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:19,754
I've never been anything but a thief,
and not a very good one.

40
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:21,069
Hey, trust me, would you?

41
00:06:21,240 --> 00:06:24,073
Now, get back to work before
I change my mind.

42
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:28,593
Hey, leave him alone.

43
00:06:30,920 --> 00:06:32,638
Go on, Eli. Get out of here.

44
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:50,032
- Hear you made parole, huh?
- What do you care?

45
00:06:51,560 --> 00:06:53,437
I just came to say goodbye.

46
00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:14,639
Drop it, Matthews.

47
00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:04,189
Now, listen.

48
00:08:04,360 --> 00:08:08,672
My newspaper is supposed
to be wrapped in plastic when it rains.

49
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:12,159
I don't care if it was clear
when you delivered it.

50
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:14,436
You can listen to the weather report.

51
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:17,160
I've been subscribing to your paper
for over 30 years

52
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:20,198
and my money's as good as
anybody else's for a dry newspaper.

53
00:08:20,360 --> 00:08:24,751
You ought to see this.
I can barely make out the front page.

54
00:08:39,560 --> 00:08:40,959
- Law offices.
<i>- Charlene.</i>

55
00:08:41,120 --> 00:08:44,396
Dad, where are you? You have
the Johnson deposition in minutes.

56
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:45,993
<i>Charlene, listen. Cancel.</i>

57
00:08:46,160 --> 00:08:49,596
- Well, we cancelled it twice last week.
<i>- Cancel it again.</i>

58
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:52,274
Do this for me:
Go in the storage room,

59
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:56,399
dig out the files on one of
my old cases, Lester Matthews.

60
00:08:56,560 --> 00:08:59,028
The Lester Matthews?
The one in the morning paper?

61
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:01,634
- Yes.
<i>- I didn't know you represented him.</i>

62
00:09:01,800 --> 00:09:02,994
I tried.

63
00:09:05,520 --> 00:09:07,238
Oh, that's the one you lost.

64
00:09:09,240 --> 00:09:12,516
- Dad?
- Just dig out the files, will you?

65
00:09:12,680 --> 00:09:14,159
I'll be there in a few hours.

66
00:09:22,760 --> 00:09:24,239
Mr. Dolan.

67
00:09:24,400 --> 00:09:27,472
- Mr. Matlock.
- Stopped by to see Les Matthews.

68
00:09:27,640 --> 00:09:29,790
Well, we've already
got a lawyer for him.

69
00:09:29,960 --> 00:09:32,679
Would you mind if I spent
a few minutes with him?

70
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:35,756
- Suit yourself.
- Thanks.

71
00:09:39,760 --> 00:09:41,398
Hey, Bob.

72
00:09:41,560 --> 00:09:44,518
Hey, what brings you out
on this miserable day?

73
00:09:44,680 --> 00:09:47,592
I understand you're holding
Les Matthews for trial.

74
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:50,797
- So?
- I'd like to see him.

75
00:09:50,960 --> 00:09:53,155
Now, you're not his lawyer.

76
00:09:53,320 --> 00:09:56,153
Bob, for old times' sake, fill me in.

77
00:09:58,240 --> 00:10:01,357
Well, a couple of inmates
witnessed a stabbing.

78
00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:05,516
When did convicted felons
become strong witnesses?

79
00:10:05,680 --> 00:10:07,955
A guard saw it too.

80
00:10:08,840 --> 00:10:10,034
You got a motive?

81
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:14,318
- I'm late for an arraignment.
- Well, Bob,

82
00:10:14,480 --> 00:10:16,550
it just doesn't make sense.

83
00:10:16,720 --> 00:10:18,278
A man a few days from parole

84
00:10:18,440 --> 00:10:20,396
committing cold-blooded murder
like that.

85
00:10:20,560 --> 00:10:23,916
That's not my problem.
And Ben, it's not yours either.

86
00:10:36,200 --> 00:10:37,838
Hello, Les.

87
00:10:43,360 --> 00:10:47,433
- What are you doing here?
- I've got a little pull with the jailer.

88
00:10:49,560 --> 00:10:54,031
Les, I know how angry you are
that I lost your case.

89
00:10:55,080 --> 00:10:57,878
Look, why don't you get out of here?
Sam already got me a lawyer.

90
00:10:58,040 --> 00:11:01,191
For what it's worth,
I never forgot your case.

91
00:11:01,360 --> 00:11:04,033
It's like an itch I can't scratch.

92
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:06,395
Somehow, somewhere,

93
00:11:07,480 --> 00:11:08,799
I missed something,

94
00:11:08,960 --> 00:11:10,951
something that would've
turned it around.

95
00:11:13,160 --> 00:11:17,915
You earned $1 00,000.
Maybe you lost a few nights' sleep.

96
00:11:18,240 --> 00:11:20,629
I've been in this joint
for seven years.

97
00:11:22,200 --> 00:11:24,191
Now get out of here.

98
00:11:26,600 --> 00:11:28,955
What I hear, you're looking at life

99
00:11:29,120 --> 00:11:31,680
unless you get the best lawyer
you can find.

100
00:11:31,840 --> 00:11:34,035
Now, I know you're angry
and I don't blame you.

101
00:11:34,200 --> 00:11:36,794
What is it with you? For seven years,
I haven't heard from you

102
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:39,349
and all of a sudden you show up
looking for work? What--?

103
00:11:39,520 --> 00:11:42,592
For seven years, there was
there was nothing I could do.

104
00:11:43,160 --> 00:11:45,355
Maybe now there is.

105
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:50,070
I'd like the chance.

106
00:11:53,840 --> 00:11:57,276
Mr. Matlock, there's a guy out there
who says he's his lawyer.

107
00:11:57,440 --> 00:11:58,953
Yeah?

108
00:12:04,400 --> 00:12:06,595
What's it going to be, Les?

109
00:12:09,360 --> 00:12:11,078
He's mistaken.

110
00:12:12,760 --> 00:12:15,354
Mr. Matlock's my attorney.

111
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:32,110
It's past your bedtime.
What are you doing here?

112
00:12:32,560 --> 00:12:34,357
I'm working on the case,

113
00:12:34,520 --> 00:12:38,308
but my toenail got caught in my sock,
so I'm working on my toenail.

114
00:12:38,480 --> 00:12:41,836
You go ahead.
I'm waiting for Tyler, anyway.

115
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:45,834
Since I got started with this,
I think I'll just finish it off.

116
00:12:50,920 --> 00:12:51,909
Go ahead.

117
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:55,760
Okay.

118
00:12:55,920 --> 00:12:59,708
- I went over the Les Matthews trial.
- A miscarriage of justice.

119
00:12:59,880 --> 00:13:01,916
Another way of saying
you hate being beaten.

120
00:13:02,080 --> 00:13:05,356
Charlene, I've got this feeling.

121
00:13:05,920 --> 00:13:08,480
Well, let's look at the facts.

122
00:13:08,640 --> 00:13:10,949
Les Matthews caught his partner
John Colter

123
00:13:11,120 --> 00:13:15,398
stealing $50,000 from their company
in order to pay off his gambling debts.

124
00:13:15,560 --> 00:13:17,152
- Right.
- The two of them

125
00:13:17,320 --> 00:13:19,515
had a violent argument,
which dozens of people saw.

126
00:13:19,680 --> 00:13:21,591
- Right.
- Later that night, Colter was found

127
00:13:21,760 --> 00:13:22,909
shot to death in his home

128
00:13:23,080 --> 00:13:25,514
and they found the murder weapon
at Matthews' house.

129
00:13:25,680 --> 00:13:28,558
- The man didn't even have an alibi.
- That's right.

130
00:13:28,720 --> 00:13:31,029
Well, now, if I'd been on the jury,
I'd have convicted.

131
00:13:31,200 --> 00:13:34,397
Well, you'd of been wrong.

132
00:13:34,560 --> 00:13:38,872
Daddy, every once in a while,
it's all right to lose.

133
00:13:39,040 --> 00:13:42,953
Charlene, these three partners
were Vietnam veterans.

134
00:13:43,120 --> 00:13:45,918
They had a dream.
They started a business.

135
00:13:46,080 --> 00:13:47,672
They were best friends.

136
00:13:47,840 --> 00:13:51,674
Les Matthews didn't kill
his best friend over $50,000.

137
00:13:51,840 --> 00:13:54,673
Even if that's true, that's not the case
we're going to try.

138
00:13:54,840 --> 00:13:58,196
- This one is a lot tougher.
- Maybe, maybe not.

139
00:13:58,360 --> 00:14:00,316
Well, you sure took your sweet time.

140
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:03,119
Well, the guy I was looking for
doesn't hang out on street corners.

141
00:14:03,280 --> 00:14:04,315
Well?

142
00:14:04,480 --> 00:14:07,233
All the news sheds a slightly
different light on this case.

143
00:14:07,400 --> 00:14:09,231
As you know, the con that got iced,

144
00:14:09,400 --> 00:14:11,994
Mr. Lashley, was doing life
for committing two murders,

145
00:14:12,160 --> 00:14:13,957
but they weren't just ordinary murders.

146
00:14:14,120 --> 00:14:15,872
They were contract hits for the mob.

147
00:14:18,560 --> 00:14:21,279
So he's not likely to kill
without getting paid.

148
00:14:21,440 --> 00:14:22,668
Not likely.

149
00:14:23,160 --> 00:14:25,151
Maybe somebody
didn't want Les Matthews

150
00:14:25,320 --> 00:14:26,958
to come out of prison alive.

151
00:14:27,120 --> 00:14:28,235
Like who?

152
00:14:28,680 --> 00:14:30,955
Maybe the same person
who put him there to start with.

153
00:14:34,640 --> 00:14:36,278
Tyler...

154
00:14:37,080 --> 00:14:38,798
...you know this is important to me.

155
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:40,678
Yes.

156
00:14:42,120 --> 00:14:45,351
We need some answers
out of prison.

157
00:14:45,920 --> 00:14:48,832
So, what do you want me to do?
Send out a questionnaire?

158
00:14:50,240 --> 00:14:54,870
Did I ever tell you that the warden
and I went to college together?

159
00:14:55,040 --> 00:14:58,316
No. Why?

160
00:15:01,640 --> 00:15:04,598
Hold it. Face forward.

161
00:15:05,160 --> 00:15:06,559
Strip.

162
00:15:10,920 --> 00:15:12,239
Hey, dimwit.

163
00:15:12,400 --> 00:15:14,436
Your sheet doesn't say
that you're deaf.

164
00:15:14,600 --> 00:15:15,749
Strip.

165
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:50,313
Hold it.

166
00:15:53,280 --> 00:15:55,111
D Block,

167
00:15:55,280 --> 00:15:59,831
C Range, Cell 1 3. You're in here.

168
00:16:00,720 --> 00:16:03,996
Hey, look, did the--
The warden talk to you about me?

169
00:16:04,160 --> 00:16:08,711
No, but if you give me any trouble,
your head will land on his desk.

170
00:16:08,880 --> 00:16:10,598
Now get in there.

171
00:16:10,760 --> 00:16:12,239
Come on.

172
00:16:20,200 --> 00:16:22,270
I'm Tyler.

173
00:16:34,080 --> 00:16:35,229
Lester.

174
00:16:35,400 --> 00:16:37,550
- Just like old times, huh?
- I hope not.

175
00:16:37,720 --> 00:16:39,358
Me too.

176
00:16:45,640 --> 00:16:47,710
Daddy,
I can't reach John Colter's widow.

177
00:16:47,880 --> 00:16:49,711
She won't return my calls.

178
00:16:49,880 --> 00:16:52,997
- Charlene Matlock.
- Hi, Les Matthews.

179
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,230
Case A981 04,

180
00:16:57,400 --> 00:17:00,551
<i>State of Georgia</i>
<i>v. Lester Matthews.</i>

181
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:02,950
Benjamin Matlock for the defence,
Your Honour.

182
00:17:03,120 --> 00:17:05,475
Defendant waives the reading
of the complaint,

183
00:17:05,640 --> 00:17:09,030
has been advised of his
constitutional rights, time is waived.

184
00:17:09,200 --> 00:17:13,751
I'm setting this case
for January 1 7th at 9 a.m.

185
00:17:17,160 --> 00:17:19,674
If this courthouse hasn't
floated away by then.

186
00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:22,993
- Prosecution agrees?
- Yes, Your Honour.

187
00:17:23,160 --> 00:17:26,072
- Mr. Matlock?
- Fine with me, Your Honour.

188
00:17:26,240 --> 00:17:30,233
Thank you, gentlemen.
I'll call the next case in just a moment.

189
00:17:30,400 --> 00:17:32,118
Les.

190
00:17:32,560 --> 00:17:33,675
Hey, Rog.

191
00:17:33,840 --> 00:17:36,638
I thought you could use
some moral support.

192
00:17:37,440 --> 00:17:40,716
Oh, Rog Bundy. This is Ben Matlock.
This is his daughter Charlene.

193
00:17:40,880 --> 00:17:42,359
- How do you do?
- Pleasure.

194
00:17:42,520 --> 00:17:45,637
Look, if there's anything you need,
please let me know.

195
00:17:45,800 --> 00:17:47,950
- I appreciate it. I gotta go.
- I'll see you in a bit.

196
00:17:51,880 --> 00:17:54,713
Actually, we met before
at Les' last trial.

197
00:17:54,880 --> 00:17:57,997
- That's right.
- You were assisting Les.

198
00:17:58,160 --> 00:17:59,593
You've got quite a memory.

199
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:02,159
Who do you assist now?

200
00:18:02,880 --> 00:18:05,872
I'm--
I'm the treasurer of the company.

201
00:18:07,280 --> 00:18:11,478
Look,
everything I've got I owe to Les.

202
00:18:11,640 --> 00:18:15,189
Mr. Matlock, you have got
to get him off this time.

203
00:18:15,640 --> 00:18:17,312
That's the plan.

204
00:18:23,080 --> 00:18:24,957
Sorry to keep you waiting.

205
00:18:25,120 --> 00:18:28,078
- Bad day, huh?
- Very.

206
00:18:29,720 --> 00:18:30,709
Les missed you in court.

207
00:18:31,800 --> 00:18:33,756
- I was busy.
- Yeah.

208
00:18:33,920 --> 00:18:36,480
My daughter says the company's
quadrupled in size.

209
00:18:36,640 --> 00:18:38,756
Well, business is great.

210
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:42,319
What can I do for you?

211
00:18:43,440 --> 00:18:46,318
Well, you're Les' partner, Mr. Dolan,

212
00:18:46,480 --> 00:18:49,677
and I was wondering
if you have any idea

213
00:18:49,840 --> 00:18:52,559
why someone would want
to keep him in prison.

214
00:18:53,560 --> 00:18:54,675
I don't follow you.

215
00:18:55,520 --> 00:18:59,638
Well, it's true that you
and John Colter's widow

216
00:18:59,800 --> 00:19:02,109
would inherit his share
of the company if he should die.

217
00:19:02,760 --> 00:19:04,193
- That's true.
- Yeah.

218
00:19:04,360 --> 00:19:07,511
Yeah, I have a theory
that the man Les stabbed

219
00:19:07,680 --> 00:19:10,877
was hired to kill him
by someone on the outside.

220
00:19:13,560 --> 00:19:16,028
I get the feeling you think it was me.

221
00:19:17,720 --> 00:19:19,676
Well, I could make a case for it.

222
00:19:19,840 --> 00:19:23,833
You live an expensive life:
houses, cars, travel.

223
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:28,039
A life you can't quite afford since
you have borrowed liberally

224
00:19:28,200 --> 00:19:31,636
from the bank account that holds
Les' share of the profits.

225
00:19:31,960 --> 00:19:33,871
Les knows all about that.

226
00:19:34,480 --> 00:19:39,315
You know, if I had a legal
and binding agreement

227
00:19:39,480 --> 00:19:42,836
that forced me to give up
large sums of money

228
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:44,513
that could've been mine

229
00:19:44,680 --> 00:19:47,831
to someone who didn't earn it
and couldn't spend it,

230
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,150
I might become bitter.

231
00:19:51,320 --> 00:19:53,629
I'm tired of you, Mr. Matlock.

232
00:19:53,800 --> 00:19:55,916
Seven years ago,
you thought I betrayed Les,

233
00:19:56,080 --> 00:19:57,638
and now it's the same thing.

234
00:19:57,800 --> 00:20:00,075
Les Matthews is my friend.

235
00:20:00,240 --> 00:20:04,870
I'd proven it time and again,
and I don't have to justify it to you.

236
00:20:05,800 --> 00:20:07,199
Well,

237
00:20:08,240 --> 00:20:11,994
that remains to be seen.
There's your subpoena.

238
00:20:17,640 --> 00:20:22,760
You know, I think Les made
a dreadful mistake hiring you.

239
00:20:23,320 --> 00:20:24,992
Well,

240
00:20:25,440 --> 00:20:28,193
maybe he thinks
I'll get it right this time.

241
00:20:43,120 --> 00:20:44,917
What the hell?

242
00:20:48,160 --> 00:20:50,037
Where is it?

243
00:20:51,120 --> 00:20:52,792
- Hey.
- Hey, what's happening here?

244
00:20:52,960 --> 00:20:54,473
- Back off, man.
- Where's the book?

245
00:20:54,640 --> 00:20:58,315
I told you I don't know.
You've gotta believe me.

246
00:21:00,200 --> 00:21:01,679
Back off.

247
00:21:04,320 --> 00:21:08,199
Hey, man. Are you all right?
Hey, come on, get up here.

248
00:21:09,760 --> 00:21:11,955
There you go.

249
00:21:12,120 --> 00:21:13,473
I've gotta get out of this place.

250
00:21:13,640 --> 00:21:15,278
Look, those guys
do not read Shakespeare.

251
00:21:15,440 --> 00:21:17,032
What kind of book
are they talking about?

252
00:21:17,200 --> 00:21:18,553
You don't want to know.

253
00:21:18,720 --> 00:21:20,119
Our cell is torn apart.

254
00:21:20,280 --> 00:21:22,430
That guy almost stabbed me
with a shiv.

255
00:21:22,600 --> 00:21:23,794
Now, I wanna know.

256
00:21:24,760 --> 00:21:27,877
Okay. Lashley, the guy who died,

257
00:21:28,600 --> 00:21:30,352
he was the top con in the joint.

258
00:21:30,880 --> 00:21:33,553
And he had this book
with all his contacts:

259
00:21:33,720 --> 00:21:38,999
drugs, payoffs,
outside sources, everything.

260
00:21:39,160 --> 00:21:40,832
Well, the book is missing.

261
00:21:42,760 --> 00:21:45,877
- You know where that book is?
- No.

262
00:22:05,800 --> 00:22:06,915
- Miss Colter?
- Yes?

263
00:22:07,080 --> 00:22:08,957
Ben Matlock.

264
00:22:09,520 --> 00:22:12,034
- Come in, Mr. Matlock.
- Thank you.

265
00:22:13,200 --> 00:22:15,953
Thanks for answering my call.

266
00:22:16,120 --> 00:22:18,429
For a while there,
I thought you were avoiding me.

267
00:22:18,600 --> 00:22:20,750
Why would I do that?

268
00:22:22,640 --> 00:22:26,633
Oh, I remember that clock.
It's beautiful.

269
00:22:26,800 --> 00:22:28,870
A clock like that should be kept up.

270
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:34,039
Oh, it probably needs repair.
Were you here before?

271
00:22:34,440 --> 00:22:39,992
Yes. I was here the day
after your husband died.

272
00:22:40,160 --> 00:22:43,948
Mr. Matlock, you know,
I resented you seven years ago

273
00:22:44,120 --> 00:22:46,190
when you defended
my husband's killer.

274
00:22:46,360 --> 00:22:48,999
And I don't really understand
what you're doing here now.

275
00:22:49,160 --> 00:22:50,229
Excuse me?

276
00:22:51,560 --> 00:22:52,834
What do you want?

277
00:22:54,480 --> 00:22:58,792
I've never been able to get your
husband's death out of my mind.

278
00:23:03,640 --> 00:23:05,039
How have you been?

279
00:23:06,800 --> 00:23:08,791
It's been difficult.

280
00:23:08,960 --> 00:23:12,077
I loved him and I miss him.

281
00:23:12,360 --> 00:23:14,920
Well, I'm-- I'm sorry.

282
00:23:15,080 --> 00:23:18,755
Seven years is a long time.
There's been no one else?

283
00:23:20,440 --> 00:23:21,634
No one like him.

284
00:23:24,480 --> 00:23:27,438
- Well, at least he left you--
- Money?

285
00:23:29,360 --> 00:23:31,430
That doesn't buy me what I want.

286
00:23:34,440 --> 00:23:36,237
Is that all you wanted to know,
Mr. Matlock?

287
00:23:38,560 --> 00:23:40,232
- Yes.
- I see.

288
00:23:40,400 --> 00:23:43,073
- And thank you.
- Goodbye, Mr. Matlock.

289
00:23:43,240 --> 00:23:48,234
Thanks. Is--
Is that today's newspaper?

290
00:23:48,400 --> 00:23:50,436
Mine got away from me,
and if you're not using it...

291
00:23:51,280 --> 00:23:53,953
No, it's not today's.

292
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:56,956
Thank you.

293
00:24:10,480 --> 00:24:12,471
Where's my book?

294
00:24:26,280 --> 00:24:27,872
It's gonna be all right, man.
You'll see.

295
00:24:28,560 --> 00:24:31,279
- You, against the wall now.
- Man-- Man, get a doctor.

296
00:24:34,640 --> 00:24:36,676
- Hold me.
- You're all right. Okay.

297
00:26:03,520 --> 00:26:05,112
What are you doing here?

298
00:26:05,280 --> 00:26:07,669
You don't live
at that other house, do you?

299
00:26:07,840 --> 00:26:10,115
Andrea, who is it?

300
00:26:11,160 --> 00:26:12,957
May I come in?

301
00:26:17,360 --> 00:26:19,794
We've been together a long time.

302
00:26:20,200 --> 00:26:21,952
How long?

303
00:26:22,120 --> 00:26:24,076
- Almost six years.
- Six years.

304
00:26:24,240 --> 00:26:28,392
We began seeing each other
about a year after John died,

305
00:26:28,560 --> 00:26:31,154
only nobody knows.

306
00:26:31,320 --> 00:26:33,595
That's why Andrea lied.

307
00:26:36,120 --> 00:26:38,509
That's a long time
for a secret like that.

308
00:26:39,440 --> 00:26:42,273
Mr. Matlock,
look at it from my point of view.

309
00:26:42,440 --> 00:26:47,468
I get Les Matthews' job,
fall in love with John Colter's widow.

310
00:26:47,640 --> 00:26:49,358
It's a little awkward.

311
00:26:50,760 --> 00:26:53,558
So you keep your home as a front.

312
00:26:53,720 --> 00:26:55,199
That's right.

313
00:26:55,360 --> 00:26:59,194
Well, if you want people
to really believe you live there,

314
00:26:59,360 --> 00:27:02,397
you'd better get some new magazines
and wind your clocks.

315
00:27:05,360 --> 00:27:07,715
Les appreciated
your coming to court.

316
00:27:08,560 --> 00:27:11,199
- You were there?
- Yes.

317
00:27:12,360 --> 00:27:17,514
I have never believed
that Les killed John Colter.

318
00:27:18,560 --> 00:27:20,516
I was in Vietnam
with the three of them.

319
00:27:20,920 --> 00:27:22,319
They were friends.

320
00:27:23,720 --> 00:27:26,632
- And that's how you know Les.
- Yes.

321
00:27:26,800 --> 00:27:28,870
And that's how he brought you
into the business?

322
00:27:29,040 --> 00:27:30,632
That's right.

323
00:27:31,200 --> 00:27:32,189
You know, it's kind of--

324
00:27:32,360 --> 00:27:36,319
It's kind of like an old movie plot.
It's interesting.

325
00:27:36,480 --> 00:27:39,517
In the movies, towards the end,
we know about the man

326
00:27:39,680 --> 00:27:43,468
and the woman and that
the man has his friend's job,

327
00:27:43,640 --> 00:27:45,790
but that's just the tip of the iceberg.

328
00:27:47,240 --> 00:27:50,915
Later, there's another revelation.

329
00:27:51,080 --> 00:27:53,594
That's how they keep your interest
to the end.

330
00:27:55,040 --> 00:27:57,952
So did you do it?

331
00:27:58,520 --> 00:28:00,397
No, I didn't do it.

332
00:28:03,120 --> 00:28:05,270
Well, I'd better be going.

333
00:28:05,440 --> 00:28:07,112
We're in court in the morning.

334
00:28:07,280 --> 00:28:09,840
- Maybe I'Il-- Maybe I'll see you there.
- Goodbye, Mr. Matlock.

335
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:11,513
Bye.

336
00:28:11,680 --> 00:28:14,240
Well, here...

337
00:28:16,640 --> 00:28:20,110
Officer Greene, on December 2nd
at precisely 3 p.m.,

338
00:28:20,280 --> 00:28:23,113
did you respond to the sounds of a
fight coming from the prison library?

339
00:28:23,280 --> 00:28:25,748
- Yes, sir, I did.
- Please tell the court what you saw.

340
00:28:26,920 --> 00:28:29,912
As I entered the library,
I saw an inmate, Mr. Matthews,

341
00:28:30,400 --> 00:28:32,789
lying over Wayne Lashley
with a shiv in his hand.

342
00:28:32,960 --> 00:28:34,109
He had just stabbed him.

343
00:28:37,400 --> 00:28:40,870
I show you People's Exhibit 5
and ask if you recognise it.

344
00:28:41,520 --> 00:28:42,509
Yes, sir, I do.

345
00:28:42,680 --> 00:28:44,875
That's the weapon I took
from Lester Matthews.

346
00:28:48,920 --> 00:28:52,310
- Nothing further.
- Cross-examine.

347
00:28:53,440 --> 00:28:55,476
Officer Green,

348
00:28:56,080 --> 00:28:59,277
you are familiar
with the defendant's prison record?

349
00:28:59,440 --> 00:29:00,793
Yes, sir. It's excellent.

350
00:29:00,960 --> 00:29:03,190
In fact, he had just won parole,
hadn't he?

351
00:29:03,360 --> 00:29:05,590
- Yes.
- In your long prison experience,

352
00:29:05,760 --> 00:29:08,638
have you ever heard of an inmate
who's just won his parole,

353
00:29:08,800 --> 00:29:11,712
committing cold-blooded murder
while he's still in prison?

354
00:29:12,240 --> 00:29:15,038
- No, sir, I haven't.
- Well, let me ask you this.

355
00:29:15,200 --> 00:29:18,192
You testified that
you saw the defendant

356
00:29:18,360 --> 00:29:20,157
holding a knife over the victim.

357
00:29:20,320 --> 00:29:22,356
- That's right.
- But you didn't actually

358
00:29:22,520 --> 00:29:24,317
see the defendant stab the victim.

359
00:29:25,440 --> 00:29:26,919
No, sir, but it looked like--

360
00:29:27,080 --> 00:29:30,072
I'm not interested in what it looked
like, Mr. Green, just what you saw.

361
00:29:30,240 --> 00:29:31,355
Now, as far as you know,

362
00:29:31,520 --> 00:29:34,512
Mr. Lashley could've attacked
Mr. Matthews.

363
00:29:34,680 --> 00:29:35,908
There could've been a struggle.

364
00:29:36,080 --> 00:29:38,435
Mr. Matthews could've killed him
in self-defence.

365
00:29:38,600 --> 00:29:43,754
- Your Honour, objection. Speculation.
- No further questions.

366
00:29:44,720 --> 00:29:48,793
Mr. Lashley went into the library
and asked Mr. Matthews for a book,

367
00:29:48,960 --> 00:29:52,748
and Mr. Matthews pulled out a knife
and stabbed him.

368
00:29:56,760 --> 00:29:57,988
Did you try and stop him?

369
00:29:58,160 --> 00:30:02,199
Yeah, I tried, but it all happened
so fast, there was nothing I could do.

370
00:30:03,720 --> 00:30:04,709
No further questions.

371
00:30:04,880 --> 00:30:05,915
Mr. Matlock?

372
00:30:06,080 --> 00:30:07,718
He's lying.

373
00:30:10,200 --> 00:30:12,031
Mr. Foley,

374
00:30:12,680 --> 00:30:16,593
did you go to the library
with Mr. Lashley and Mr. Lipton?

375
00:30:16,760 --> 00:30:17,749
Yeah, that's right.

376
00:30:17,920 --> 00:30:20,150
Well, why did the three of you
go to the library?

377
00:30:21,720 --> 00:30:23,358
- To get some books.
- I see.

378
00:30:24,560 --> 00:30:27,757
Was there something in particular
you were interested in reading?

379
00:30:29,120 --> 00:30:30,758
Well, I was gonna look around.

380
00:30:31,440 --> 00:30:37,629
How about, would you mind reading
that right there to the court?

381
00:30:38,160 --> 00:30:40,469
- Yeah, I would.
- Objection, Your Honour.

382
00:30:40,640 --> 00:30:42,995
No relevancy. I fail to see the point
of this demonstration.

383
00:30:43,160 --> 00:30:46,869
- You will in a second.
- Mr. Foley, please read the book.

384
00:30:47,520 --> 00:30:49,272
There you go.

385
00:30:51,960 --> 00:30:53,029
I can't.

386
00:30:53,200 --> 00:30:54,394
- You can't?
- No.

387
00:30:54,880 --> 00:30:56,472
Are you saying you can't read?

388
00:30:56,640 --> 00:30:58,790
Yeah. So what? I read the pictures.

389
00:31:00,760 --> 00:31:03,877
Isn't it true you did not go to the library
for a book?

390
00:31:04,040 --> 00:31:05,155
- No.
- Isn't it true

391
00:31:05,320 --> 00:31:07,550
you were one of Wayne Lashley's
errand boys?

392
00:31:07,720 --> 00:31:08,835
- No.
- Isn't it true

393
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:11,070
he ordered you
to guard the library door

394
00:31:11,240 --> 00:31:13,037
while he attempted to kill
Les Matthews?

395
00:31:13,200 --> 00:31:14,553
That's a bunch of bull--

396
00:31:15,520 --> 00:31:19,308
Isn't it true you're serving a life term for
armed robbery and a double murder

397
00:31:19,480 --> 00:31:21,994
and perjury means nothing to you?

398
00:31:22,680 --> 00:31:23,954
No further questions.

399
00:31:24,120 --> 00:31:25,917
Redirect?

400
00:31:27,240 --> 00:31:28,275
No, Your Honour.

401
00:31:28,800 --> 00:31:30,791
Ben, level with me, will you?

402
00:31:30,960 --> 00:31:32,871
We're not getting anywhere, are we?

403
00:31:33,040 --> 00:31:34,951
Well, we're making some points,

404
00:31:35,120 --> 00:31:37,998
and my people
are working day and night.

405
00:31:39,640 --> 00:31:40,868
Thanks.

406
00:31:41,760 --> 00:31:43,796
Oh, that would be very helpful.

407
00:31:43,960 --> 00:31:45,234
If you--

408
00:31:46,240 --> 00:31:49,118
Okay, as long as it's someone
that you know and that's reliable.

409
00:31:49,280 --> 00:31:51,874
All right. Thanks a lot. Bye.

410
00:31:52,040 --> 00:31:53,712
Dad?

411
00:31:55,840 --> 00:31:57,432
What?

412
00:31:57,640 --> 00:32:00,837
- You look terrible.
- Thanks.

413
00:32:01,160 --> 00:32:03,116
Daddy, I'm gonna call
a CP in the morning.

414
00:32:03,280 --> 00:32:04,872
I've been all through
these company books.

415
00:32:05,040 --> 00:32:07,508
Sam Dolan made them a fortune.

416
00:32:07,680 --> 00:32:09,750
Now, I'm no expert,
but somehow, someway,

417
00:32:09,920 --> 00:32:12,718
there's a lot less money here
than there ought to be.

418
00:32:14,760 --> 00:32:15,795
Did Tyler call?

419
00:32:16,320 --> 00:32:17,833
Not yet.

420
00:32:19,960 --> 00:32:21,871
Hope he's all right.

421
00:32:29,920 --> 00:32:32,593
Hey, take the bottom bunk.

422
00:32:32,760 --> 00:32:34,239
Here.

423
00:32:35,800 --> 00:32:37,870
Say, I owe you. You saved my life.

424
00:32:38,040 --> 00:32:40,998
You wanna repay me?
Give me that book.

425
00:32:42,080 --> 00:32:44,594
I don't trust anyone here.

426
00:32:45,120 --> 00:32:46,917
You can trust me.

427
00:32:50,520 --> 00:32:53,318
Eli, you're gonna
have to trust someone.

428
00:33:05,720 --> 00:33:07,438
Bundy.

429
00:33:08,120 --> 00:33:09,758
Hello?

430
00:33:09,920 --> 00:33:11,399
Hello?

431
00:33:14,800 --> 00:33:16,392
Well...

432
00:33:17,320 --> 00:33:19,675
...now we know Roger Bundy's
private number

433
00:33:19,840 --> 00:33:22,434
was in Wayne Lashley's
little black book.

434
00:33:22,960 --> 00:33:25,793
Well, that still doesn't prove
he paid Lashley to kill Les.

435
00:33:26,480 --> 00:33:28,436
- I know.
- Now, wait a minute.

436
00:33:28,600 --> 00:33:32,275
You mean I spent all that time
in prison, risked my life for nothing?

437
00:33:32,440 --> 00:33:33,509
Not exactly.

438
00:33:34,400 --> 00:33:38,234
If Mr. Bundy's our boy,
he didn't commit one murder.

439
00:33:38,400 --> 00:33:39,628
He committed two.

440
00:33:56,480 --> 00:33:58,471
- Mr. Matlock.
- Yes, ma'am.

441
00:34:01,440 --> 00:34:03,317
Thank you
for agreeing to see me here.

442
00:34:03,480 --> 00:34:05,994
You sounded so urgent
on the phone.

443
00:34:06,160 --> 00:34:10,711
Well, it was a night just like tonight
that your husband was murdered.

444
00:34:10,880 --> 00:34:13,474
- It was raining.
- That's right.

445
00:34:13,640 --> 00:34:15,756
Now, what was so urgent,
Mr. Matlock?

446
00:34:15,920 --> 00:34:19,117
You and Mr. Bundy didn't tell me
the whole truth when we spoke.

447
00:34:20,160 --> 00:34:23,470
- We didn't?
- No. A couple of things.

448
00:34:23,640 --> 00:34:25,551
Six weeks before your husband died,

449
00:34:25,720 --> 00:34:28,917
you and Mr. Bundy
were in Bermuda at the same time.

450
00:34:29,080 --> 00:34:31,674
In fact, you were on the same airplane.
Was that a coincidence?

451
00:34:33,560 --> 00:34:36,393
Why did you lie
about when the affair began?

452
00:34:37,840 --> 00:34:40,832
I thought that we should tell everybody
the truth.

453
00:34:41,000 --> 00:34:42,956
Exactly what is that?

454
00:34:45,440 --> 00:34:48,716
My testimony at Les Matthews'
trial was true.

455
00:34:49,360 --> 00:34:51,920
I did come home
and find John's body.

456
00:34:52,080 --> 00:34:55,038
The house was dark.
It was thundering.

457
00:34:55,200 --> 00:34:57,156
- But when--
- You called the police?

458
00:34:57,320 --> 00:34:59,834
I was about to call the police.

459
00:35:00,000 --> 00:35:02,355
Roger arrived.

460
00:35:02,520 --> 00:35:04,511
And he was drenched from the rain.

461
00:35:05,720 --> 00:35:10,510
He said that he had-- Had a drink
earlier in the day with Les Matthews

462
00:35:10,680 --> 00:35:15,800
and that he'd come over
to talk with John.

463
00:35:16,240 --> 00:35:19,152
He was hoping to make peace
between Les and John.

464
00:35:20,600 --> 00:35:23,910
Why didn't you tell anyone
he was here?

465
00:35:25,480 --> 00:35:27,471
Because if anyone knew
we were having an affair,

466
00:35:27,640 --> 00:35:29,039
we would've looked guilty.

467
00:35:30,640 --> 00:35:32,915
I want you to think back.

468
00:35:34,080 --> 00:35:36,753
Did you see his car come up?

469
00:35:36,920 --> 00:35:39,115
My husband had just been killed.

470
00:35:39,280 --> 00:35:42,033
But you waited for the car to leave
to call the police?

471
00:35:44,040 --> 00:35:45,678
Yes, I did.

472
00:35:45,840 --> 00:35:48,035
- Did you see the car?
- No.

473
00:35:48,200 --> 00:35:50,191
Roger wasn't in the habit
of parking near the house

474
00:35:50,360 --> 00:35:51,588
when he came to see me.

475
00:35:51,760 --> 00:35:53,432
But that night
he didn't come to see you.

476
00:35:53,600 --> 00:35:55,477
He came to see your husband.

477
00:35:55,640 --> 00:35:58,552
Now, I want you to tell me
exactly what happened when he left.

478
00:36:00,120 --> 00:36:03,999
He walked to the door,
gave me a kiss

479
00:36:04,160 --> 00:36:08,517
and said not to worry and left.

480
00:36:08,960 --> 00:36:10,359
Did you watch him go?

481
00:36:10,520 --> 00:36:12,715
Yes. I could see his umbrella
go down the street.

482
00:36:13,720 --> 00:36:15,756
And that was it,
you called the police?

483
00:36:16,880 --> 00:36:19,030
Mr. Matlock, no matter what we did,

484
00:36:19,200 --> 00:36:21,839
I know Les Matthews
killed my husband.

485
00:36:22,000 --> 00:36:25,913
Well, it's taken seven years,

486
00:36:26,080 --> 00:36:28,071
but I think
I'm gonna prove you wrong.

487
00:36:31,480 --> 00:36:34,677
Come to court tomorrow.
It might be interesting.

488
00:36:39,280 --> 00:36:42,511
How long have you known
Les Matthews?

489
00:36:42,680 --> 00:36:44,398
About 1 7 years.

490
00:36:44,560 --> 00:36:46,676
In fact, you served
in Vietnam with him, didn't you?

491
00:36:46,840 --> 00:36:48,558
- Yes, I did.
- Now, when was that?

492
00:36:49,360 --> 00:36:51,555
1 970 to '7 1 .

493
00:36:51,720 --> 00:36:55,110
And when Les Matthews
came home in 1 97 1 ,

494
00:36:55,280 --> 00:36:57,077
you stayed on for another tour,
didn't you?

495
00:36:57,240 --> 00:36:58,229
Yes.

496
00:36:58,400 --> 00:36:59,913
According to your military record,

497
00:37:00,080 --> 00:37:04,790
you were in the 3rd Battalion
of the 1 01 st Airborne Division.

498
00:37:04,960 --> 00:37:06,518
- Right?
- That's right.

499
00:37:07,440 --> 00:37:10,830
Did you know a fellow
named Wayne Lashley?

500
00:37:11,600 --> 00:37:13,556
- No, I didn't.
- Are you sure?

501
00:37:13,720 --> 00:37:14,914
Yes.

502
00:37:15,080 --> 00:37:16,354
The reason I sound sceptical

503
00:37:16,520 --> 00:37:19,751
is because I have his military record
right here,

504
00:37:20,840 --> 00:37:25,595
and during 1 97 1 and '72,
he served in your outfit.

505
00:37:26,440 --> 00:37:28,078
You sure you didn't know him?

506
00:37:28,240 --> 00:37:29,559
I just told you I didn't.

507
00:37:30,160 --> 00:37:33,516
Okay. Let me ask you this then.

508
00:37:34,080 --> 00:37:38,471
You're the treasurer and chief financial
officer of the Dacoma Corporation.

509
00:37:38,640 --> 00:37:40,198
- Is that right?
- That's right.

510
00:37:40,360 --> 00:37:42,794
And you have a private telephone
in your office?

511
00:37:42,960 --> 00:37:43,949
Yes.

512
00:37:44,120 --> 00:37:50,559
And let's see, the number is 555-6960.
Is that right?

513
00:37:50,720 --> 00:37:52,312
Yes.

514
00:37:52,480 --> 00:37:54,710
I bet you wonder
how I got that number.

515
00:37:54,880 --> 00:37:56,393
Well, I'll tell you.

516
00:37:56,560 --> 00:37:59,074
I got it out of this little black book.

517
00:37:59,240 --> 00:38:03,916
And oddly enough, this book
was owned by Wayne Lashley.

518
00:38:04,080 --> 00:38:09,074
Now, how do you suppose
your private, unlisted number

519
00:38:09,240 --> 00:38:12,949
wound up in this convicted murderer's
telephone book?

520
00:38:13,120 --> 00:38:14,439
I don't know.

521
00:38:14,600 --> 00:38:17,831
- You still insist you didn't know him.
- That's right.

522
00:38:18,840 --> 00:38:22,150
I won't belabour the point.
We'll move on to something else.

523
00:38:22,320 --> 00:38:26,950
Seven years ago when your friend,
the defendant, Les Matthews,

524
00:38:27,120 --> 00:38:32,353
went to prison, you took over his job
as chief financial officer

525
00:38:32,520 --> 00:38:34,511
of the Dacoma Corporation, right?

526
00:38:34,680 --> 00:38:35,715
That's right.

527
00:38:35,880 --> 00:38:39,270
Now in that time,
the business has done very well.

528
00:38:39,440 --> 00:38:41,158
So have you.

529
00:38:41,640 --> 00:38:44,552
I have bank statements that show

530
00:38:44,720 --> 00:38:49,669
that you have accumulated
$4.2 million in six different accounts.

531
00:38:51,760 --> 00:38:54,911
Well, we...

532
00:38:55,080 --> 00:38:58,117
We have some excellent
investment portfolios.

533
00:39:00,880 --> 00:39:04,839
You know,
since I defended Les Matthews

534
00:39:05,000 --> 00:39:08,879
on a charge of murder
seven years ago,

535
00:39:09,040 --> 00:39:13,318
I've always felt like I missed
something, some kind of thread.

536
00:39:13,920 --> 00:39:18,198
Well, Mr. Bundy,
you have done me the great service

537
00:39:18,360 --> 00:39:22,478
of showing me I didn't miss anything.

538
00:39:22,640 --> 00:39:25,108
Seven years ago,
there was nothing to miss.

539
00:39:25,280 --> 00:39:27,589
There was nothing to find.

540
00:39:27,800 --> 00:39:31,110
But now, I found it.

541
00:39:31,280 --> 00:39:34,909
Your Honour,
I'd like to enter into evidence

542
00:39:35,080 --> 00:39:36,991
this statement
and supporting documents

543
00:39:37,160 --> 00:39:41,597
from a certified public accountant
showing that in the past seven years,

544
00:39:41,760 --> 00:39:47,278
exactly $4.2 million have been stolen
from the Dacoma Corporation

545
00:39:47,440 --> 00:39:49,635
by financial manipulation
of its books.

546
00:39:50,280 --> 00:39:51,759
You stole that money, didn't you?

547
00:39:51,920 --> 00:39:53,069
No.

548
00:39:53,240 --> 00:39:56,437
You killed John Colter so you
could steal that money, didn't you?

549
00:39:56,600 --> 00:39:58,556
- No.
- Objection.

550
00:39:58,760 --> 00:40:01,354
We are here to try Les Matthews
for the murder of Wayne Lashley.

551
00:40:01,520 --> 00:40:05,035
We are not here to retry a murder
he has already been found guilty of.

552
00:40:05,200 --> 00:40:07,634
Your Honour,
may we approach the bench?

553
00:40:12,800 --> 00:40:16,509
Your Honour, I believe I have evidence
showing that these two murders

554
00:40:16,680 --> 00:40:18,511
are inextricably linked together.

555
00:40:18,680 --> 00:40:21,990
But Your Honour, as I just said,
Les Matthews has already be--

556
00:40:22,160 --> 00:40:24,879
Burton.
Burton don't you want to find the truth?

557
00:40:25,040 --> 00:40:27,031
Yes, Ben. Of course I do,
but the past is the past.

558
00:40:27,200 --> 00:40:29,077
Gentlemen,
no fighting in this courtroom.

559
00:40:29,240 --> 00:40:32,391
Your Honour,
I honestly believe this is the only way

560
00:40:32,560 --> 00:40:34,391
we'll ever discover the truth.

561
00:40:35,560 --> 00:40:40,076
Well, very well, Mr. Matlock,
but I warn you: Get to the point.

562
00:40:40,240 --> 00:40:42,879
Objection overruled.
Proceed, Mr. Matlock.

563
00:40:43,040 --> 00:40:48,114
Mr. Bundy, isn't it true that you
were having an affair with Mrs. Colter

564
00:40:48,280 --> 00:40:51,272
for almost a year
before her husband was murdered?

565
00:40:51,440 --> 00:40:52,429
Yes.

566
00:40:52,600 --> 00:40:54,875
In fact, only a moment
after she discovered the body,

567
00:40:55,040 --> 00:40:57,110
you came into her house, didn't you?

568
00:40:58,240 --> 00:40:59,753
- Yes.
- And you both decided

569
00:40:59,920 --> 00:41:03,595
it would be wise for you to leave
before she called the police.

570
00:41:03,760 --> 00:41:06,069
- Yes.
- Why was that?

571
00:41:06,800 --> 00:41:12,158
Because we thought
we might be considered suspects.

572
00:41:12,760 --> 00:41:14,591
When you came into the house,
you were drenched

573
00:41:14,760 --> 00:41:17,672
as if you had been
standing out in the rain unprotected.

574
00:41:17,840 --> 00:41:19,478
It was raining very hard.

575
00:41:19,640 --> 00:41:23,553
And yet when you left,
you had your umbrella.

576
00:41:26,320 --> 00:41:29,596
Now, Mrs. Colter
will be glad to testify that you did.

577
00:41:32,240 --> 00:41:34,515
What was your umbrella
doing there?

578
00:41:37,080 --> 00:41:39,674
I don't know. I...

579
00:41:40,040 --> 00:41:43,749
I must've left it there
a couple of days before.

580
00:41:44,400 --> 00:41:45,992
Well,
you don't usually carry an umbrella

581
00:41:46,160 --> 00:41:47,957
on a warm sunny day, do you?

582
00:41:48,120 --> 00:41:49,758
I suspect not.

583
00:41:49,920 --> 00:41:52,309
And you're sure you left it there
a couple of days before?

584
00:41:52,480 --> 00:41:54,755
Couldn't have been a week,
maybe two?

585
00:41:56,560 --> 00:42:00,439
Well, you know, it might have been
a couple of weeks before.

586
00:42:01,280 --> 00:42:03,077
Mr. Bundy,

587
00:42:03,840 --> 00:42:08,277
there are two things
I remember about August 1 979:

588
00:42:08,720 --> 00:42:11,837
One was John Colter's murder.

589
00:42:12,000 --> 00:42:14,958
The other, my garden died.

590
00:42:15,680 --> 00:42:17,511
We had a drought that summer.

591
00:42:19,080 --> 00:42:24,757
Your Honour, I have a headline here
from the <i>Atlanta Examiner</i>

592
00:42:24,920 --> 00:42:31,189
dated August 1 0th,1 979, the morning
after John Colter was murdered.

593
00:42:31,360 --> 00:42:32,952
And the headline reads, and I quote:

594
00:42:33,120 --> 00:42:38,240
''Surprise storm
ends eight-week-Iong drought.''

595
00:42:40,480 --> 00:42:45,508
Now, why was your umbrella
at the Colter house during a drought?

596
00:42:45,680 --> 00:42:50,515
You know, those warm sunny days
we have here in Atlanta.

597
00:42:57,920 --> 00:43:00,718
I don't know. I...

598
00:43:00,880 --> 00:43:03,792
It probably
was some kind of a mistake.

599
00:43:03,960 --> 00:43:05,916
I'll tell you why.

600
00:43:07,560 --> 00:43:11,314
You went to see John Colter
before his wife came home

601
00:43:11,480 --> 00:43:13,835
the night of August 9th.

602
00:43:14,000 --> 00:43:15,991
<i>It was raining,</i>
<i>you had your umbrella.</i>

603
00:43:16,160 --> 00:43:19,709
<i>You shot him.</i>
<i>You heard someone drive up.</i>

604
00:43:19,880 --> 00:43:22,952
<i>You ran out the French doors.</i>
<i>No umbrella.</i>

605
00:43:23,120 --> 00:43:24,633
You got drenched.

606
00:43:24,800 --> 00:43:28,156
Then you saw it was Andrea
who had come home.

607
00:43:28,320 --> 00:43:32,836
Then you realised
you forgot your umbrella.

608
00:43:33,000 --> 00:43:35,958
So you went around
to the front of the house,

609
00:43:36,120 --> 00:43:37,519
came in the front door--

610
00:43:37,680 --> 00:43:39,716
You killed him! You killed John!

611
00:43:39,880 --> 00:43:42,394
- No, I didn't.
- Yes, you did.

612
00:43:43,120 --> 00:43:47,636
Seven years ago,
you saw your opportunity to clean up.

613
00:43:47,800 --> 00:43:50,678
You knew Sam Dolan
couldn't add a column of figures.

614
00:43:50,840 --> 00:43:54,435
So when Les Matthews
got in a fight with John Colter,

615
00:43:54,600 --> 00:43:57,717
you saw your opportunity
to get rid of both of them

616
00:43:57,880 --> 00:43:59,791
and steal to your heart's content.

617
00:44:00,680 --> 00:44:03,069
But when Les Matthews
won his parole,

618
00:44:03,240 --> 00:44:06,198
you knew he'd come home
and discover your thefts.

619
00:44:06,360 --> 00:44:10,319
So you contacted your old army buddy,
Wayne Lashley,

620
00:44:10,480 --> 00:44:12,710
and you hired him to kill
Les Matthews.

621
00:44:12,880 --> 00:44:17,476
Only it backfired.
My client killed him in self-defence.

622
00:44:17,960 --> 00:44:20,679
Isn't that the way it happened?

623
00:44:22,640 --> 00:44:24,198
No.

624
00:44:25,840 --> 00:44:28,479
I believe with the help of Mrs. Colter,

625
00:44:28,640 --> 00:44:33,316
the good officers of the
Atlanta Police Department will prove it.

626
00:44:37,200 --> 00:44:39,350
The defence rests.

627
00:44:44,320 --> 00:44:47,118
Ladies and gentlemen of the jury,
have you reached a verdict?

628
00:44:47,280 --> 00:44:48,554
We have, Your Honour.

629
00:44:48,720 --> 00:44:54,352
We find the defendant,
Lester Matthews, not guilty.

630
00:44:55,480 --> 00:44:58,597
- Thank you.
- Don't thank me. Thank him.

631
00:44:58,760 --> 00:45:01,797
- Yeah. Thank me.
- Thank you very...

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
